concepts.content:
While backpropagation is often described for a single datapoint, the total cost function typically represents the average of losses over all datapoints in a training set, $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$. Because the gradient of a sum is the sum of the gradients, we can compute the gradient for the whole dataset using the identity:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\partial J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta}
\end{aligned}$$

To compute a gradient update for algorithms like stochastic gradient descent, we apply backpropagation in batch mode by running it over each datapoint in the batch (often in parallel) and then averaging the results.

